    {
      "identifier": "gold_anderson_1989_table1_thermodynamic_inputs",
      "kind": "heterogeneous_thermodynamic_compilation",
      "description": "The 20 printed Table I rows of heat capacity, volume expansivity, and ultrasonic elastic modes assembled by Anderson et al. from seven earlier sources. These are property-level inputs to a staged thermodynamic construction, not P-V-T observations sharing a regression objective.",
      "reference": {
        "authors": ["Anderson", "Isaak", "Yamamoto"],
        "year": 1989,
        "title": "Anharmonicity and the equation of state for gold",
        "source": "J. Appl. Phys.",
        "volume": "65",
        "locator": "1534-1543",
        "doi": "10.1063/1.342969"
      },
      "source_location": "Section II, Equations (4)-(5), and Table I, page 1535",
      "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.342969",
      "columns": [
        {"name": "temperature_k", "quantity": "temperature", "unit": "K", "role": "value"},
        {"name": "elastic_series", "quantity": "source_series", "unit": "1", "role": "flag"},
        {
          "name": "cp_printed_0p1_j_g_k",
          "quantity": "specific_heat_capacity_at_constant_pressure",
          "unit": "0.1 J g^-1 K^-1",
          "role": "value"
        },
        {
          "name": "cp_reconstructed_0p1_j_g_k",
          "quantity": "specific_heat_capacity_at_constant_pressure",
          "unit": "0.1 J g^-1 K^-1",
          "role": "value"
        },
        {
          "name": "volume_expansivity_1e6_k_inverse",
          "quantity": "volume_thermal_expansivity",
          "unit": "10^-6 K^-1",
          "role": "value"
        },
        {"name": "c11_prime_gpa", "quantity": "adiabatic_elastic_mode_c11_prime", "unit": "GPa", "role": "value"},
        {"name": "c44_gpa", "quantity": "adiabatic_elastic_constant_c44", "unit": "GPa", "role": "value"},
        {"name": "c_prime_gpa", "quantity": "adiabatic_elastic_mode_c_prime", "unit": "GPa", "role": "value"},
        {"name": "cp_source", "quantity": "source_identifier", "unit": "1", "role": "flag"},
        {"name": "expansivity_source", "quantity": "source_identifier", "unit": "1", "role": "flag"},
        {"name": "elastic_source", "quantity": "source_identifier", "unit": "1", "role": "flag"}
      ],
      "resource": {
        "path": "datasets/gold-anderson-1989-table1-thermodynamic-inputs.csv",
        "sha256": "6a460edad1925133feb9edbf2d0bad9d27dd1677a2e14821fb0f3a65f174253e",
        "media_type": "text/csv"
      },
      "used_by_eos_records": ["gold_anderson_1989_bm3_1"],
      "uncertainty": {
        "elastic_constants": "The paper states 0.5% accuracy for the measured elastic constants; the source studies report about 1% uncertainty for their temperature derivatives.",
        "other_properties": "No row-wise uncertainty or covariance is printed."
      },
      "provenance": {
        "type": "transcribed_from_primary_source_table",
        "source_artifact_sha256": "38a4a6276270c3777d20c0437e22103612c32f5c0b622ebba32f3532499f9024",
        "upstream_sources": [
          "Hultgren et al. (1963)",
          "Barin and Knacke (1973)",
          "White and Collins (1972)",
          "AIP Handbook (1972)",
          "Touloukian et al. (1975)",
          "Neighbours and Alers (1958)",
          "Chang and Himmel (1966)"
        ]
      },
      "notes": "The two 300 K elastic rows are retained separately. Table I visibly prints Cp=1.1288 at 300 K. That number breaks the smooth sequence and yields gamma=3.395 through Equation (12), whereas Table IV prints gamma=2.974. Equation (4) gives 1.28820 and inversion of Equation (12) with the printed Table III Ks and Table IV gamma gives 1.28843, so cp_reconstructed_0p1_j_g_k records 1.2888 as the evident intended value while preserving the literal print in the adjacent column. Low-temperature expansivities were obtained by graphical smoothing and are not independently reproducible from the article alone."
    },
